    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "You can now mark a client as tax exempt, and their invoices then carry no tax whatever the lines or tax rate say. `bin/e2e` builds and the acceptance test passes: a tax-exempt client's invoice at 20% tax shows tax `$0.00` and a total of `$200.00`. I didn't check the new checkboxes and labels by hand beyond what that test covers.\n\nThe task description called the server OfficeFloor, but it's a Spring Boot app (`net.officefloor` is only the package name), so I wrote it in Spring like the existing code. I copied the pattern the app already uses for the \"prices include tax\" setting.\n\n- **Database:** a new migration, `V40__client_tax_exempt.sql`, adds a tax-exempt flag to both clients and invoices. Existing clients and invoices default to not exempt, so no existing amounts change.\n- **Each invoice keeps its own copy of the flag**, taken from the client when the invoice is created. Changing a client's exemption updates only their draft invoices; sent invoices keep the figures they were issued with.\n- **The levy is zeroed too**, not just sales tax, since it's a second tax and you said \"no tax\". On an exempt invoice the taxable amount is $0 and the total is the subtotal less any discount.\n- **Server:** clients now accept and return the flag, and the invoice detail returns it. The test-data seeding endpoint also accepts `taxExempt` on clients and leaves out tax when working out the stored amount.\n- **Front end:**\n  - A \"Tax exempt (no tax on any invoice)\" checkbox on the add-client and edit-client forms (`client-form-tax-exempt`, `client-edit-form-tax-exempt`).\n  - A \"Tax exempt: Yes/No\" line on the client page (`client-tax-exempt`).\n  - A note on exempt invoices saying no tax is charged (`invoice-tax-exempt`).\n\n  I didn't rename or remove any existing `data-testid`s.",
